## My Journey (so far)

I started my career by teaching myself how to build both software and hardware - programming PIC microcontrollers with C. Since then, I've worked as a local and international freelancer through Upwork, built products in fintech with companies like Cardvest, and led engineering at a health-tech company, Innovate Health Africa. I currently work as a Senior Software Engineer at Selar. Along the way, I've also founded and built my own products.

*Note: this is the "highlight reel" version - there's more underneath it that hasn't made it into any bio yet.*

---

## Why I Mentor (the real answer, not the interview answer)

I learned on my own, with no guide. I wasted precious time tinkering and finding my way around - things that could've taken 3 months took closer to a year because there was no one to point the way.

I don't want someone else to go through that same waste.

Over the years I've trained students directly - including students who didn't believe they could be in tech at first. Many of my own mentees are now working in great companies across Nigeria and a few outside the country.

I enjoy passing on knowledge, helping people cross bridges that seemed difficult, and helping them avoid the avoidable mistakes.

**The core of it:** I simply want to see my mentees rise, and know I played a part in their journey.

*(Context: I noticed I've been asked "why do you mentor?" in interviews before and given a generic answer instead of this one - because I'd forgotten it myself in the moment. This section exists so that doesn't happen again.)*

